non-u	From non- + U ("characteristic of the upper classes"), coined by British linguist Alan S. C. Ross (1907-1980) in an article in 1954, and popularized by the English journalist and writer Nancy Mitford (1904-1973).
non-white	From non- + white.
non_compos_mentis	From Latin non ("not") + compos ("in control, having mastery") + mentis ("of mind").
non_prosequitur	Latin [Term?], meaning "he does not prosecute".
non_sequitur	Learned borrowing from Latin nōn sequitur (literally "it does not follow").
