nonautonomous	From non- + autonomous.
nonbearing	From non- + bearing.
nonbeing	From Middle English nounbeyng, noon being, noon beyng, equivalent to non- + being.
nonbeliever	From non- + believer.
nonbelligerent	From non- + belligerent.
noncaloric	From non- + caloric.
noncandidate	From non- + candidate.
noncarbonated	From non- + carbonated.
noncausal	From non- + causal.
noncausative	From non- + causative.
nonce	Inherited from Middle English nonse, nones, a rebracketing of Middle English to þen anes, for þen anes (to/for the once (i.e., the one occasion or instance)), from the dative singular neuter of þe. The cryptography sense is commonly said to be a contraction of number used once, although this is probably incorrect.	1975. Unknown, derived from British criminal slang. Several origins have been proposed; possibly derived from dialectal nonce, nonse ("stupid, worthless individual") (but this cannot be shown to predate nonce "child-molester" and is likely a toned-down usage of the same insult), or Nance, nance ("effeminate man, homosexual"), from nancy or nancyboy. The rhyme with ponce has also been noted. As prison slang also said to be an acronym for "Not On Normal Communal Exercise" (Stevens 2012), but this is likely a backronym.
nonce_word	From for the nonce and word. Coined by James Murray, the influential first editor of the Oxford English Dictionary.
noncellular	From non- + cellular.
nonchalance	Borrowed from French nonchalance.
nonchalant	Borrowed from French nonchalant, from Old French nonchaloir ("to be unconcerned"), from non- ("not") + chaloir ("to have concern for"), from Latin non ("not") + calēre ("to be warm").
nonchalantly	From nonchalant + -ly.
