unitarian	Related to New Latin ūnitārius (from Latin ūnitās ("unity")) -an. First documented as unitaria religio, in a decree of the Diet of Lécfalva (1600). In English since 1687	From unitary + -ian.
unitarianism	From unitarian + -ism.	From Unitarian + -ism.
unitary	From unit + -ary.
unite	From Middle English uniten, from Latin ūnītus, perfect passive participle of ūniō.
united	From unite + -ed.
united_arab_emirates	Calque of Arabic الْإِمَارَات الْعَرَبِيَّة الْمُتَّحِدَة (al-ʔimārāt al-ʕarabiyya l-muttaḥida).
united_kingdom	From united + kingdom, first attested from 1706.
united_kingdom_of_great_britain_and_northern_ireland	Derived from United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland, following the Irish Independence and the Partition of Ireland in 1922, leaving Northern Ireland to stay in the United Kingdom.
united_mexican_states	Calque of Spanish Estados Unidos Mexicanos
united_nations	(international coalition): Ellipsis of United Nations Organization.
united_states_of_america	First attested in 1776, see names of the United States.
united_states_virgin_islands	Named by Christopher Columbus after Saint Ursula and her virgin followers.
unitedly	From united + -ly.
unitise	From unit + -ise.
unitization	From unitize + -ation.
unitize	From unit + -ize.
unity	From Middle English unite, from Anglo-Norman, Old French unité, from Latin ūnitās, from ūnus ("one") + noun of state suffix -itās, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *óynos ("one, single"), hence distantly related to one and an. By surface analysis, unite + -y. Displaced native Old English ānnes (literally "oneness").	Majuscule letter version of unity.
univalent	From uni- + -valent.
univalve	Originated 1655-65 from uni- ("one") + valve.
universal	From Middle English universal, from Old French universal (modern French universel), from Latin ūniversālis, equivalent to universe + -al.
universalise	From universal + -ise.
universalism	From universal + -ism.
universalist	From universal + -ist.
universalistic	From universalist + -ic or universal + -istic.
universality	From Middle English universalite, from Middle French universalité, from Old French, from Late Latin universalitas. By surface analysis, universal + -ity.
universalize	From universal + -ize.
universally	From Middle English universally; equivalent to universal + -ly.
universe	From Middle English universe, from Old French univers, from Latin universum ("all things, as a whole, the universe"), neuter of universus ("all together, whole, entire, collective, general, literally turned or combined into one"), from uni-, combining form of unus ("one") + versus ("turned"), perfect passive participle of vertō ("to turn"). Analyzable as uni- + -verse through backformation of -verse.	From Middle English, directly or via Old French univers, from Latin universum. See universe.
university	From Middle English universite ("institution of higher learning, body of persons constituting a university") from Anglo-Norman université, from Old French universitei, from Medieval Latin stem of universitas, in juridical and Late Latin "a number of persons associated into one body, a society, company, community, guild, corporation, etc"; in Latin, "the whole, aggregate," from universus ("whole, entire"). By surface analysis, universe + -ity.
univocal	From Late Latin ūnivocus + -al. By surface analysis, uni- + vocal.
unix	A pun on Multics to sound like eunuchs, as Unix was considered to be an emasculated Multics (see multi- and uni-). Likely coined by Canadian computer scientist Brian Kernighan in 1970.
unjointed	From un- + jointed.
unjust	From un- + just.
unjustifiable	From un- + justifiable.
unjustifiably	From unjustifiable + -ly.
unjustified	From un- + justified.
unjustly	From unjust + -ly or un- + justly.
unjustness	From unjust + -ness.
unkempt	From earlier unkembed, unkemmed, from Middle English unkempt ("uncombed"), equivalent to un- + kempt. Compare Old Norse úkembdr (uncombed; unkempt"; whence Icelandic ókembdur), German ungekämmt ("unkempt"), Dutch ongekamd. More at kemb.
unkemptness	From unkempt + -ness.
unkept	From un- + kept.	Reanalysis of obsolete kempt to kept, keep in the sense "maintain (a coiffure)". Compare uncombed.
